What upstream lacks (hermes-agent, apps/desktop): the main process's Node REST
client (bearer requests, the remote dashboards' native sign-in) sent no
Accept-Encoding and could not decode a compressed body, so nginx's gzip on
the dashboards never applied to the app. The phone apps got ~3x smaller
history pages and 4-7x smaller session lists; the desktop did not (Dave's
laptop on a 4G hotspot, 2026-11-27: 189 MB in 5.5 h, all uncompressed).

This patch (Nix): fetchJson asks for gzip unless the caller named an
encoding, and decodes a gzip/x-gzip body; any other encoding passes
through unchanged. A corrupt body rejects once and drops the socket.

Tests: electron/api-transport-gzip.test.ts.

